CH = dict(num=9, title="The Amazing World of Solutes, Solvents and Solutions", theme="Solutions, solubility, density",
 intro="Every sip of homemade ORS tastes the same because sugar and salt spread evenly through the water. This chapter explains **solutions** (uniform mixtures), how much solute a solvent can hold (**solubility**, saturated and unsaturated solutions), how temperature changes it, and then **density** — the property that decides, with other factors, whether things float or sink — including how to measure mass, volume and density.",
 big_questions=["What happens when you add too much sugar to tea and it stops dissolving? How can you solve this?", "Why do sugar and salt dissolve in water but not in oil? Why is water a good solvent?", "Why are water bottles usually tall and cylindrical instead of spherical?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "9.1 Solute, Solvent and Solution"),
 ("table", ["Mixture", "Type", "Why"], [
   ["Sugar + salt + water (ORS)", "Uniform", "Components evenly distributed, every sip same"],
   ["Chalk powder / sand / sawdust + water", "Non-uniform", "Components not evenly spread; can be seen"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **solution** is a uniform mixture. When a solid dissolves in a liquid, the solid is the **solute** and the liquid is the **solvent**: *solute + solvent → solution*. In a solution of **two liquids**, the one in **smaller amount is the solute** and the one in larger amount is the solvent."),
 ("ul", ["Components of a solution cannot be seen separately; in non-uniform mixtures they can be seen with the eye or a magnifier.",
         "A mixture of gases (such as air) is also a uniform mixture, i.e. a solution.",
         "**Gulab jamun chashni:** a large amount of sugar in a small amount of water — still water is the solvent and sugar the solute."]),
 ("h2", "9.2 How Much Solute Can a Fixed Amount of Solvent Dissolve?"),
 ("steps", [
   ("Activity 9.1 — Add salt to water", "Half-fill a tumbler with water; add one spoon of salt, stir till dissolved, keep adding spoon by spoon and stir. Record in a table whether each spoon dissolves."),
   ("Observation", "At first salt dissolves completely; after a few spoons some **settles at the bottom**. Water has reached its **limit** at that temperature.")]),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning"], [
   ["**Unsaturated solution**", "More solute can still be dissolved at that temperature"],
   ["**Saturated solution**", "No more solute dissolves at that temperature; extra settles at the bottom"],
   ["**Concentration**", "Amount of solute present in a fixed quantity of solution (or solvent)"],
   ["**Dilute solution**", "Relatively less solute"],
   ["**Concentrated solution**", "Relatively more solute (dilute and concentrated are *relative* terms)"],
   ["**Solubility**", "Maximum amount of solute that dissolves in a fixed quantity (100 mL / 100 g) of solvent at a given temperature"]]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "4 spoons of salt in 50 mL of water is **more concentrated** than 2 spoons in 100 mL — compare the solute per same volume."),
 ("h3", "9.2.1 Effect of temperature on solubility"),
 ("steps", [
   ("Activity 9.2 — Baking soda in water (demonstration)", "Take about 50 mL of water at ~20 °C and add baking soda until some is left undissolved. Heat to 50 °C, stirring: the undissolved soda dissolves. Add more until some remains, heat to 70 °C: it dissolves again."),
   ("Inference", "Water at 70 °C dissolves more than at 50 °C, which dissolves more than at 20 °C. For **most solids, solubility increases with temperature**; a saturated solution becomes **unsaturated** when heated.")]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Scientific heritage:** water is the main solvent in Ayurveda and Siddha formulations; hydro-alcoholic extracts, oils, ghee and milk are also used as solvents. **Asima Chatterjee** developed anti-epileptic and anti-malarial drugs by extracting compounds from medicinal plants with solvents; she was the second Indian woman to earn a Doctorate of Science, first woman to get the Shanti Swarup Bhatnagar Award in chemical science, and received the Padma Bhushan."),
 ("h2", "9.3 Solubility of Gases"),
 ("ul", ["Many gases, including oxygen, dissolve in water (a uniform mixture), but only to a small extent. This **dissolved oxygen sustains aquatic life** — plants, fishes and others.",
         "**For gases, solubility generally decreases as temperature increases.** Cold water holds more oxygen; when water warms, less oxygen stays dissolved, which can harm aquatic life."]),
 ("table", ["Solute", "Effect of rising temperature on solubility in liquid"], [["Solids (most)", "**Increases**"], ["Gases", "**Decreases**"]]),
 ("h2", "9.4 Why Do Objects Float or Sink in Water?"),
 ("p", "Husk floats while rice sinks when rice is washed; oil floats on water; sawdust floats but sand sinks. Generally objects lighter than the liquid float and heavier ones sink; the special property that describes this ‘heaviness’ is **density**. Iron feels heavier than a wooden stick of the same size because it is denser. (Density is not the only factor deciding floating or sinking.)"),
 ("h2", "9.5 What Is Density?"),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "**Density = Mass ÷ Volume.** Density is the mass present in a unit volume of the substance. It does **not depend on shape or size**, but it **depends on temperature and pressure** (pressure matters mainly for gases)."),
 ("ul", ["Analogy: a crowded bus is high density; a dense forest has trees close together.",
         "**SI unit:** kg/m<sup>3</sup>. Other units: g/mL and g/cm<sup>3</sup> (liquids and solids).",
         "**Conversion:** 1 kg/m<sup>3</sup> = 1000 g/m<sup>3</sup> = 1 g/L = 1 g/1000 mL = 1 g/1000 cm<sup>3</sup> (so 1 g/cm<sup>3</sup> = 1000 kg/m<sup>3</sup>).",
         "1 mL of water has a mass of about 1 g (at room temperature), so 10 mL water ≈ 10 g and 100 mL ≈ 100 g.",
         "**Relative density** = density of substance ÷ density of water at that temperature; it has **no units**. Aluminium: 27 g in 10 cm<sup>3</sup> gives 2.7 g/cm<sup>3</sup>, i.e. relative density 2.7 (2.7 times as dense as water).",
         "Oil labelled 1 litre but 910 g shows its density is **less than water**, so oil floats."]),
 ("h3", "9.5.1 Determining density: measure mass and volume"),
 ("table", ["Quantity", "Meaning", "Unit / instrument"], [
   ["**Mass**", "Quantity of matter in an object", "g, kg; balance"],
   ["**Weight**", "Force with which Earth attracts an object", "newton (N)"],
   ["**Volume**", "Space occupied", "m<sup>3</sup>, cm<sup>3</sup> (=cc), dm<sup>3</sup>; litre (L) = 1 dm<sup>3</sup>; mL = 1 cm<sup>3</sup>"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Most balances (except the **two-pan balance**) actually measure weight but are marked in mass units. Mass is constant; weight changes with place."),
 ("steps", [
   ("Activity 9.3 — Measure mass", "Switch on a digital balance, bring it to zero using the **tare** button, place a clean watch glass (or butter paper), tare again, put the stone on it and read its mass (e.g. 16.400 g). For a liquid, use a beaker instead of the watch glass."),
   ("Activity 9.4 — Know your measuring cylinder", "Maximum volume = largest mark (e.g. 100 mL). **Least count** = volume between two big marks ÷ number of small divisions (10 mL ÷ 10 = **1 mL**). Typical least counts: 0.1 mL for 10 and 25 mL cylinders, 1 mL for 100 mL, 2 mL for 250 mL, 5 mL for 500 mL."),
   ("Choosing the cylinder", "To measure 70 mL: a 50 mL cylinder needs two steps (inconvenient); 250 or 500 mL cylinders do it in one step but with lower accuracy; a **100 mL cylinder is best**."),
   ("Activity 9.5 — Measure 50 mL of water", "Place the cylinder on a flat surface, pour slowly to the mark, adjust with a dropper, keep the **eye level with the bottom of the meniscus** (the curved water surface) and read at its bottom. For **coloured liquids read the top** of the meniscus."),
   ("Activity 9.6 — Regular solid", "Volume of a cuboid = l × w × h (notebook 25 × 18 × 2 = **900 cm<sup>3</sup>**)."),
   ("Activity 9.7 — Irregular solid (displacement)", "Take 50 mL water (initial volume A), tie the stone with thread and lower it slowly, note the final level B (55 mL). **Volume of object = B − A = 5 mL = 5 cm<sup>3</sup>.** Then density = 16.400 g ÷ 5 cm<sup>3</sup> = **3.28 g/cm<sup>3</sup>**.")]),
 ("ul", ["Measuring cylinders are **tall and narrow** so that a small change in volume produces a large, easily read change in height (better accuracy than a wide beaker).",
         "Earth’s layers (crust, upper mantle, lower mantle, outer core (liquid), inner core (solid)): **density increases towards the centre** because pressure and temperature rise and material becomes compact. The crust is lightest.",
         "**Heritage:** bamboo and wooden logs, being light and hollow, were used for rafts and boats in ancient times."]),
 ("h3", "9.5.2 Effect of temperature on density"),
 ("p", "On heating, particles move apart, **volume increases but mass stays the same**, so density = mass/volume **decreases**. Cooling raises density. Hence **hot air rises** (less dense than the cool air around it) — the principle of the **hot air balloon**."),
 ("h3", "9.5.3 Effect of pressure on density"),
 ("table", ["State", "Effect of increasing pressure"], [
   ["Gas", "Particles move closer; volume decreases, **density increases** (large effect)"],
   ["Liquid", "Small effect — nearly incompressible"],
   ["Solid", "Even less effect; change usually negligible"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Why ice floats:** water’s density is **highest at 4 °C**. When water freezes at 0 °C its particles arrange in a structure taking **more space (expansion)**; same mass, larger volume, lower density, so ice is lighter and floats. The ice layer on top insulates the water below, keeping fish and aquatic animals alive in freezing weather."),
 ("p", "**Egg test:** a raw egg sinks in tap water; dissolving enough **salt** in the water increases the liquid’s density until the egg floats. Peeled orange sinks but unpeeled floats because the peel traps air and lowers overall density."),
 ("h2", "Quick Questions with Model Answers"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Define solute, solvent and solution.", "A solution is a uniform mixture; the dissolved substance is the solute and the dissolving medium is the solvent."),
  ("In a solution of two liquids, which is the solute?", "The one present in the smaller amount."),
  ("Distinguish saturated and unsaturated solutions.", "Unsaturated can dissolve more solute at that temperature; saturated cannot, and extra solute settles."),
  ("What is solubility? How does temperature affect it?", "Maximum solute that dissolves in a fixed quantity of solvent at a temperature; for solids it generally increases, for gases it decreases."),
  ("Why do fishes die in warm water?", "Dissolved oxygen decreases as temperature rises."),
  ("State the formula and SI unit of density.", "Density = mass/volume; kg/m3 (g/cm3 also used)."),
  ("Find the volume of an irregular stone.", "Displacement method: final level minus initial level of water in a measuring cylinder."),
  ("A stone sculpture of 225 g has volume 90 cm3. Will it float?", "Density = 2.5 g/cm3, greater than 1 g/cm3, so it sinks."),
  ("Why does hot air rise?", "On heating, volume increases while mass is constant so density falls; it is lighter than surrounding cool air."),
  ("Why does ice float on water?", "Ice has a more spread-out structure, so lower density than liquid water."),
  ("What is meniscus and how do you read it?", "Curved liquid surface; eye at level, read bottom for colourless liquids and top for coloured liquids.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["A **solution** is a uniform mixture of **solute** and **solvent**.",
  "**Saturated** solution cannot dissolve more solute at that temperature; **unsaturated** can. Solubility of **solids rises** and of **gases falls** with temperature.",
  "**Density = mass / volume** (kg/m<sup>3</sup>, g/cm<sup>3</sup>); relative density has no unit.",
  "Volume of irregular solids is found by **displacement of water**; read the **meniscus** correctly.",
  "Heating **decreases** density; pressure greatly **increases** gas density, but barely affects solids and liquids.",
  "**Ice floats** because water expands on freezing; water is densest at **4 °C**."])
